{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What does a while loop do?",
      "options": [
        "Repeats its lines as long as its condition is true",
        "Runs its lines a number of times given up front",
        "Checks its condition once, then runs its lines to the end",
        "Repeats its lines until the program ends"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "When does a while loop check its condition?",
      "options": [
        "Only once, before the first pass",
        "Before every pass, the first one too",
        "After every pass, so its lines always run at least once",
        "Only when the variable in the condition changes"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "What is the last number this prints?",
      "code": "i = 0\nwhile i < 10:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1",
      "options": ["10", "11", "9", "0"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "Why doesn't `while i < 10:` print 10, when i grows by 1 from 0?",
      "options": [
        "Python always skips the last number of a loop",
        "10 is printed too, but after the loop",
        "The loop stops one pass early to save time",
        "When i is 10, i < 10 is false, so the loop stops"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "i starts at 0 and grows by 1. Which line prints every number from 0 to 10, both included?",
      "options": ["while i <= 10:", "while i < 10:", "while i == 10:", "while i > 10:"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "i = 0\nwhile i <= 100:\n    print(i)",
      "options": [
        "It prints every number from 0 to 100",
        "It prints 0 forever, since i never changes",
        "It prints 0 once, then stops",
        "It stops with an error: the loop has no i = i + 1"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "What do we call a loop whose condition never becomes false?",
      "options": ["A for loop", "An empty loop", "An infinite loop", "A nested loop"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "Which line keeps `while i < 5:` from running forever, when i starts at 0?",
      "options": [
        "i = 0, before the loop",
        "print(i), inside the loop",
        "i = i + 1, after the loop",
        "i = i + 1, inside the loop"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "How many times does this print Hi?",
      "code": "i = 10\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(\"Hi\")\n    i = i + 1",
      "options": [
        "Never: 10 < 5 is false from the start",
        "Once: the lines run before the check",
        "5 times",
        "Forever"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "When does a while loop fit better than a for loop?",
      "options": [
        "When we count from 1 to 10",
        "When we don't know up front how many times to repeat",
        "When the loop has a print() in it",
        "Never: a for loop can always do it more simply"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "Why does the lesson write `password = \"\"` before the loop?",
      "options": [
        "So the password is empty at the end",
        "Because input() needs an empty variable",
        "So the condition has something to check on the first pass",
        "So the loop runs only once"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "`password` was never made. What happens when Python reaches `while password != \"starlet\":`?",
      "options": [
        "Python skips the loop",
        "The loop runs forever",
        "password becomes \"\" by itself",
        "It stops with an error"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "What does `!=` mean in `while password != \"starlet\":`?",
      "options": ["Is not equal to", "Is equal to", "Gets a new value", "Is less than or equal to"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "In the lesson's password example the user types moon, then starlet. How many times does the program ask for the password?",
      "options": ["Once", "Twice", "Three times", "Until the user stops the program"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "In the lesson's password example, when does the loop stop?",
      "options": [
        "As soon as the user types anything",
        "After three wrong tries",
        "When password is starlet, so password != \"starlet\" is false",
        "When the password is empty"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "In the Access Code mission the user types 1111, then 2222, then 1234. What does the program print?",
      "options": [
        "Wrong code\nAccess granted",
        "Access granted",
        "Wrong code\nWrong code\nWrong code\nAccess granted",
        "Wrong code\nWrong code\nAccess granted"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "In the Access Code mission, why is the code compared with \"1234\", in quotes?",
      "options": [
        "input() gives back text, and the text \"1234\" is not the number 1234",
        "Quotes make the comparison faster",
        "A while line needs quotes in its condition",
        "So 1234 is printed at the end"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q18",
      "text": "In the Access Code mission the user types 1234 right away. What does the program print?",
      "options": [
        "Wrong code, then Access granted",
        "Only Access granted",
        "Nothing",
        "Wrong code, again and again"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q19",
      "text": "What has to come at the end of a while line?",
      "options": [";", ".", ":", ","],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q20",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "i = 0\nwhile i < 3:\n    print(i)\ni = i + 1",
      "options": [
        "It prints 0, 1 and 2",
        "It prints 0, 1, 2 and 3",
        "It stops with an error",
        "It prints 0 forever: i = i + 1 is after the loop"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21",
      "text": "What is the last number this prints?",
      "code": "i = 3\nwhile i > 0:\n    print(i)\n    i = i - 1",
      "options": ["1", "0", "3", "-1"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "How many times do the loop's lines run?",
      "code": "n = 0\nwhile n < 10:\n    n = n + 2",
      "options": ["10", "5", "4", "6"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "Which program prints the same as `for i in range(5):` with `print(i)` inside?",
      "options": [
        "i = 0\nwhile i <= 5:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1",
        "i = 1\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1",
        "i = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1",
        "i = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    i = i + 1\n    print(i)"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q24",
      "text": "In `while i < 10:`, what is `i < 10`?",
      "options": [
        "How many times the loop runs",
        "The value i starts from",
        "A comment for the loop",
        "The condition: the loop runs as long as it is true"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q25",
      "text": "Which of these lines is right?",
      "options": ["while i < 10:", "while i < 10", "while: i < 10", "while i < 10 do:"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q26",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "total = 0\ni = 1\nwhile i <= 4:\n    total = total + i\n    i = i + 1\nprint(total)",
      "options": ["4", "10", "6", "15"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q27",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "i = 5\nwhile i < 5:\n    print(i)\n    i = i + 1\nprint(\"Done\")",
      "options": ["5\nDone", "Nothing", "Done", "5"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q28",
      "text": "A while loop and a for loop print the same numbers. Why does the while one need more lines?",
      "options": [
        "A while loop is slower, so it needs more lines",
        "A for loop can't print numbers on their own",
        "There is no difference: they always have as many lines",
        "We make the variable before it and change it inside it ourselves; for does both itself"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q29",
      "text": "For a while loop to stop, which variable has to change inside it?",
      "options": [
        "The one printed in the loop",
        "One that its condition checks",
        "Any variable of the program",
        "None: a while loop stops by itself after a while"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q30",
      "text": "What does Python do once the condition of a while loop is false?",
      "options": [
        "It skips the loop's lines and goes on with the line after the loop",
        "It starts the loop again from the top",
        "It stops the program with an error",
        "It runs the loop's lines one more time"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    }
